{
  "dom_id": "step-q93891",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Consider the Diophantine equation $9x+11y=6.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the greatest possible negative value of $y.$ What is the value of $\\overset{∼}{y}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=9,$ $b=11,$ and $c=6.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(9,11)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}11 & = & 9\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 9 & = & 2\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =11-9 \\\\ 1 & =9-2\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =9-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =9-4(11-9) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5+11\\cdot (-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n9\\cdot 5+11\\cdot (-4)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 5+11\\cdot (-4) & =1 \\\\ 9\\cdot 5\\cdot 6+11\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 6 & =6 \\\\ 9\\cdot 30+11\\cdot (-24) & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(30,-24)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(30+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-24-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(30+11t,\\,-24-9t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe greatest possible negative value of $y=-24-9t$ is attained when $t=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{y}=-24-9\\cdot (-2)=-6.\n$$",
